## Antumbra torrc for the system tor on Android.
##
## render-torrc.sh replaces @ANTUMBRA_DIR@ with the runtime directory
## (/data/adb/antumbra on the phone). The module starts tor as uid/gid 2950
## with 'tor -f <this file>'. tor is built with --disable-system-torrc, so it
## reads no other configuration file.
##
## What the module creates before it starts tor (tor checks the owner and
## mode of its own directories and refuses to start otherwise):
##   @ANTUMBRA_DIR@            owner root, mode 0711, a tmpfs mounted fresh
##                             at every boot, so guard choices, time stamps
##                             and logs never reach flash.
##   @ANTUMBRA_DIR@/state      owner root, mode 0711.
##   @ANTUMBRA_DIR@/state/tor  owner 2950:2950, mode 0700.
##   @ANTUMBRA_DIR@/run        owner 2950, group 2950 or 0, mode 0751 or
##                             0750 (0751 lets uid 2951 open status.json).
##   @ANTUMBRA_DIR@/log        owner root, mode 0755. tor cannot create
##                             files there, so it cannot plant a link that
##                             root's own log writes would follow.
##   @ANTUMBRA_DIR@/log/tor.log  an existing plain file, owner 2950:2950,
##                             mode 0600, which root creates before every
##                             start of tor; tor only appends to it.
## Every directory above them (/data/adb, @ANTUMBRA_DIR@) must be
## searchable (x) by uid 2950.

## --- Where tor keeps its state ------------------------------------------
DataDirectory @ANTUMBRA_DIR@/state/tor
## Write state and cache files as rarely as possible.
AvoidDiskWrites 1
## Stay in the foreground; the module supervises the process.
RunAsDaemon 0
## No 'User' line: the module starts tor directly as uid 2950 with every
## capability dropped. tor never runs as root, even briefly.

## --- What tor offers to the rest of the phone -----------------------------
## No SOCKS port. Apps reach Tor only through the firewall's redirect to
## TransPort, so every name lookup goes through the DNS filter. A SOCKS port
## would let an app send host names to Tor directly, past the filter.
SocksPort 0
## Transparent proxy: the firewall redirects all TCP from every uid except
## 2950 here. Connections to different destination addresses use separate
## circuits. IsolateClientAddr is tor's default and is stated for clarity;
## after the redirect every app has the same client address, so it does not
## separate apps from each other (per-app circuits are later work).
TransPort 127.0.0.1:9040 IsolateClientAddr IsolateDestAddr
## Name lookups: only the DNS filter (uid 2951) may reach this port. It
## listens on 127.0.0.1:5353 and forwards what it does not block here.
DNSPort 127.0.0.1:9053
## Answer lookups of .onion and .exit names with addresses from
## VirtualAddrNetworkIPv4, which TransPort maps back to the name.
AutomapHostsOnResolve 1
AutomapHostsSuffixes .onion,.exit
VirtualAddrNetworkIPv4 10.192.0.0/10
## Refuse connections to private and local addresses (the router, the LAN)
## and drop lookup answers that point at them. These are tor's defaults;
## they are stated because the LAN must stay unreachable through Tor too.
ClientRejectInternalAddresses 1
ClientDNSRejectInternalAddresses 1

## --- Control interface ------------------------------------------------------
## No TCP control port: any app can open a loopback TCP port, and the
## control port can point tor at an attacker's bridge. The control socket is
## a Unix socket with mode 0660, owned by uid/gid 2950: only root (the
## module) and tor's own uid can use it. RelaxDirModeCheck lets the run
## directory be 0751, so the DNS filter can read status.json there.
ControlPort 0
ControlSocket unix:@ANTUMBRA_DIR@/run/tor-control.sock GroupWritable RelaxDirModeCheck
CookieAuthentication 1
CookieAuthFile @ANTUMBRA_DIR@/run/tor-control.authcookie
CookieAuthFileGroupReadable 0

## --- Network ------------------------------------------------------------------
## Start with the network off. After the Welcome screen the module sends
## one SETCONF with any bridges and DisableNetwork=0 over the control
## socket, unless the person chose to stay offline; if tor refuses any part
## of it, nothing changes and the network stays off. While the network is
## off, tor holds no TransPort or DNSPort (tor's design): 127.0.0.1:9040
## and :9053 are free, and the module must check who holds them.
DisableNetwork 1
## IPv4 only. IPv6 is switched off on the phone and dropped by the firewall.
ClientUseIPv4 1
ClientUseIPv6 0
ClientPreferIPv6ORPort 0

## --- Logging ------------------------------------------------------------------
## Notices only, to a file in RAM. Nothing goes to logcat, which other
## system components and adb can read. SafeLogging (tor's default) keeps
## what the person visits (stream destinations, client addresses) out of
## the log, but not the addresses of the relays and bridges tor connects
## to: a warning about a relay or bridge names its IP address and port. So
## tor.log can hold the person's bridges, and it must never be shown or
## exported as it is: not on the status page, not in the Welcome app, not
## in a CI artifact from a run with real bridges.
Log notice file @ANTUMBRA_DIR@/log/tor.log
SafeLogging 1
## Same as Tails: do not warn about the POP3 and IMAP ports, so people are
## not trained to ignore warnings about mail clients that use STARTTLS.
WarnPlaintextPorts 23,109

## --- What Tails' torrc does that this file keeps or drops --------------------
## Kept:
##   TransPort on 127.0.0.1:9040 with IsolateDestAddr; AutomapHostsOnResolve
##   with the .exit and .onion suffixes; DNSPort (moved to 9053, because the
##   DNS filter takes Tails' 5353); cookie authentication; AvoidDiskWrites 1;
##   WarnPlaintextPorts 23,109; DisableNetwork 1 until the person has made
##   their choice at the Welcome screen.
## Changed:
##   Tails' TransPort also has IsolateDestPort; Antumbra isolates by
##   destination address only, to keep the number of circuits on a phone
##   down. Logs go to a file instead of the systemd journal. Debian's
##   'User debian-tor' becomes a dedicated Android uid (2950) chosen by the
##   module; Debian's group-readable cookie becomes owner-only.
## Dropped:
##   All four SocksPorts (9050, 9062, 9063 and the browser's 10.200.1.1:9050):
##   Tor Browser for Android runs its own tor, and a SOCKS port would bypass
##   the DNS filter. ControlPort 127.0.0.1:9052: replaced by the control
##   socket. Sandbox 1: tor's seccomp sandbox needs libseccomp and is written
##   for glibc's system calls; this build has no sandbox (on the phone, tor is
##   instead confined by its uid, dropped capabilities and the firewall).
